WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.170
INTRO:
Since the start of the pandemic, physical

00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:04.270
retailers have gotten crushed.

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:08.010
Given the fear of mingling with the public,
the appeal of physical locations has dwindled

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:12.929
over the past year, causing dozens of legacy
players to file for bankruptcy including JCPenney,

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:17.520
J. Crew, Century 21, Guitar Center, Pier 1,
and many more.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:24.269
But, one retailer who has not only survived
the pandemic but has gone parabolic is Dillards.

00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:28.510
Since the pandemic lows, Dillards was up a
maximum of 1409%.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:34.550
That’s nearly 3 times their previous peak
and nearly 100% more than Tesla’s parabolic

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:35.550
run.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:39.230
Not only has their stock gone to the moon,
but Dillards also has the fundamentals to

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:40.640
back up this run.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.960
Dillards has been posting record profits and
their PE ratio is still under 10.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.940
So, here’s how one of the oldest and slowest
moving companies was able to outperform even

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.420
the most innovative companies in the world
since the pandemic started.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:58.890
CONTROLLING THE REAL ESTATE
If you’re not familiar with Dillard’s,

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:02.840
they’re a very popular department store
in the US who specializes in clothing and

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:03.840
fashion.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:07.750
While I’m sure they have their own fanbase
that won't shop anywhere else, as far as the

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:11.890
general public is concerned, Dillard’s is
just another department store like Macy’s

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:13.120
or Nordstrom.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:17.180
This isn’t necessarily a bad thing as most
of the competition doesn’t have anything

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.270
special going for them on the consumer side
either.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:23.330
But, unlike much of the competition, Dillard’s
has figured out a way to address this issue

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:24.830
from the business side.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:28.490
You see, one of the biggest shortfalls of
running a retail store is the massive overhead

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:30.460
that comes along with each store.

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:34.030
You have to pay for leases, maintenance, and
dozens of employees regardless of whether

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:35.659
you make sales or not.

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:39.159
And given that retail stores don’t have
the highest net margins, even the smallest

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:42.020
downturn in demand can leave you unprofitable.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:45.650
This isn’t anything new though, and Dillard’s
has been strategically combating this shortfall

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:47.500
by owning their stores.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:53.240
As of 2011, Dillards owned 241 out of their
309 stores, many of which they transferred

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.430
into a real estate investment trust or REIT.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:02:00.310
And today, this number is simply even higher
with Dillard’s owning 90% of their retail

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:01.310
space.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:04.520
Just like us mortals, owning large amounts
of real estate as a retailer is a massive

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:05.520
advantage.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.220
Your monthly payments are building equity,
your real estate is appreciating, you get

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:13.430
tax write offs for interest and depreciation,
and most importantly, you have full control

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:14.440
over your property.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:18.480
If a downturn like the pandemic does hit,
and you need to close some stores, you can

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.040
cash in on your equity or you can become a
landlord yourself.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:25.349
On the other hand, if you were leasing, you’re
gonna have to negotiate to end your lease

00:02:25.349 --> 00:02:28.379
early and probably pay hefty fines.

00:02:28.379 --> 00:02:32.570
All of the most successful retailers today
including Walmart and The Home Depot own most

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:33.680
of their stores.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.970
And they’ve all been taking the real estate
side of the business extremely seriously for

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:38.400
quite some time now.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.930
On the flipside, most of the dying retailers
don’t own their real estate or they were

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:43.100
late to the game.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:47.500
JCPenney, for example, didn’t form a REIT
until they filed for bankruptcy last year.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:51.700
Clearly, owning their stores has been a great
advantage for Dillard’s, but this isn’t

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.680
their only cost saving measure either.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.960
LEANING UP THE BUSINESS
Another way Dillard’s has been able to establish

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.370
some stability in the cutthroat retail industry
is leaning up the business.

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:06.780
Historically, Dillard’s has been known for
being a higher end retailer carrying brands

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:09.989
like Calvin Klein, Coach, Ralph Lauren, and
Michael Kors.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:13.930
But, over the past few years, Dillard’s
has focused on appealing to the middle class

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.580
and lower income families as well through
their clearance stores.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:20.209
As the name suggests, these clearance stores
specialize in selling items that weren’t

00:03:20.209 --> 00:03:25.400
able to be sold in regular Dillards, and these
stores have some of the best deals out there.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.720
Before sending merchandise to these clearance
stores, Dillard’s tries to price these items

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.930
60% off at their regular stores.

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:35.019
And these items only make their way to clearance
stores if they still don’t sell.

00:03:35.019 --> 00:03:38.459
This means that only the most undesirable
stuff makes its way to clearance centers,

00:03:38.459 --> 00:03:43.569
but on the brightside, these items are often
70 to 80% cheaper by the time they make it

00:03:43.569 --> 00:03:44.569
there.

00:03:44.569 --> 00:03:47.880
Dillard’s currently operates 30 clearance
centers across the US, which has really expanded

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:51.420
Dillard’s target audience and helped them
survive a time period in which many consumers

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.599
were more frugal than ever before.

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:57.450
Aside from appealing to lean customers, Dillard’s
has become much leaner themselves.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:02.489
Dillard’s owns 100% of their corporate headquarters,
distribution centers, and fulfillment facilities

00:04:02.489 --> 00:04:04.459
which has minimized overhead.

00:04:04.459 --> 00:04:07.909
Dillards has also been strategically closing
a handful of stores that weren’t performing

00:04:07.909 --> 00:04:09.129
as well.

00:04:09.129 --> 00:04:13.150
Thanks to these cost cutting measures, Dillard’s
didn’t have to furlough or lay off any employees

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:16.760
during the pandemic and their balance sheet
has been remarkably healthy.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:20.890
Their debt to equity ratio currently stands
at 0.4 meaning that they have more equity

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:22.919
than debt on all their assets.

00:04:22.919 --> 00:04:29.070
Also, their long term debt is only $599 million
and their next payment isn’t due till January

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.070
of 2023.

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:34.139
Considering that their net income over the
past 12 months itself is $608 million, Dillard’s

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:36.070
shouldn’t have any trouble handling this
debt.

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:40.790
As you can see, Dillard’s has really strong
fundamentals, but fundamentals alone aren’t

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:43.870
responsible for Dillard’s exponential rise.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:48.430
MEME STOCKS
Some of the most explosive runs originate

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:52.340
from heavily shorted stocks as shorts exit
their positions, and it’s no different with

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:53.470
Dillards.

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:57.550
Once the pandemic rolled around, countless
hedge funds went all in on shorting recovery

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:58.550
stocks.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:03.330
You know, the restaurants, the cruise lines,
the airlines, the hotels, and of course the

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:04.330
retailers.

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:06.810
Most of these bets played out very well as
many of these businesses straight up filed

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:07.810
for bankruptcy.

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:12.250
But, among these short sided plays, the hedge
funds overlooked some gems such as Dillard’s,

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:14.310
and this would come back to haunt them.

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:17.970
As y’all probably already know, we saw a
massive rise in heavily shorted stocks near

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.150
the beginning of 2021.

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:24.930
Most of the momentum and hype went into GameStop
and AMC, but naturally, many investors who

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:27.570
had missed out on those plays flocked to other
heavily shorted stocks.

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:33.560
We saw dozens of posts about DDS pop up on
Wall Street Bets, and DDS was able to recover

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:36.240
to pre pandemic levels through the GameStop
rise.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:39.810
But it wasn’t until the second round of
short squeezes led by AMC that Dillards truly

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:40.810
went parabolic.

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:45.810
Now, the SEC claims that the rise in AMC and
GameStop was mostly fueled by momentum as

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:47.460
opposed to an actual short squeeze.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:51.850
But regardless of the specific reason, the
AMC and Gamestop runs were extremely beneficial

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:53.180
to Dillards.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:57.160
Unlike most meme stocks that stalled out after
the momentum died though, Dillards has continued

00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:00.169
their exponential run thanks to the supply
shortage.

00:06:00.169 --> 00:06:04.770
BALLOONING MARGINS
Most of us associate the supply shortage with

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:09.450
technology and lumber, but the supply shortage
has also carried into the fashion industry

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:10.450
as well.

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:14.729
Now, Dillard’s hasn’t exactly pumped up
prices to the moon like Nvidia, but they have

00:06:14.729 --> 00:06:18.830
been able to heavily cut back on discounts
which carries directly into their bottom line.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:22.970
For most of the 2010s, Dillard’s net margin
has hovered at just under 5%.

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:27.630
Right before the pandemic, Dillard’s net
margins were actually declining towards 2%,

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:29.810
and it of course went negative during the
pandemic.

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:33.949
But since the start of 2021, Dillard’s margins
have been able to sharply recover and reach

00:06:33.949 --> 00:06:36.130
record levels of 10%.

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:39.310
Most analysts didn’t expect such numbers
whatsoever.

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:44.169
The analyst consensus for 3rd quarter earnings
was $1.93 per share, but Dillard’s came

00:06:44.169 --> 00:06:49.060
in with an astounding $9.81 which is 5 times
the expected amount.

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:53.930
As you would guess, this is the primary factor
behind Dillard’s recent 100% run in November.

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:58.270
Dillard’s has given back most of those gains
over the past few weeks, but many argue that

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:00.380
this is simply a great buying opportunity.

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:04.820
Dillard’s has proven strong resilience from
the pandemic, they control a lot of real estate,

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:06.470
and they have pretty strong financials.

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:11.069
Also, even though their revenue has recovered
significantly, it’s not even back to pre

00:07:11.069 --> 00:07:12.289
pandemic levels yet.

00:07:12.289 --> 00:07:17.110
Currently, they’re about 5-10% away from
pre pandemic levels, and given that a lot

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:20.870
of the competition has dropped out since the
pandemic, it’s very possible that Dillard’s

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:23.230
is able to outgrow their former self.

00:07:23.230 --> 00:07:28.889
It should also be noted that even after a
14x run, Dillard’s is only worth $5.5 billion.

00:07:28.889 --> 00:07:32.830
Meanwhile, Lululemon pulls in roughly the
same profit, but they're worth 10 times more

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:34.690
coming in at $53 billion.

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:39.120
Dillard’s doesn’t have nearly as much
hype as Lululemon, but considering Lululemon’s

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:43.470
valuation, it’s not crazy to think that
Dillards could grow to a 15 to $20 billion

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:44.470
valuation.

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:47.629
At the same time though, many aren’t nearly
as convinced by Dillard’s.

00:07:47.629 --> 00:07:52.940
UNCERTAIN FUTURE
Here’s the thing, Dillard’s may have strong

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:58.379
fundamentals, but at the end of the day, they’re
still just a strong company in a dying industry.

00:07:58.379 --> 00:08:02.840
Year after year, malls are seeing less and
less foot traffic, and people are becoming

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:05.160
more and more willing to buy everything online.

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:09.819
Dillard’s is also decently competitive on
the online side of things, but if ecommerce

00:08:09.819 --> 00:08:14.259
is the future, it makes little sense to operate
such massive stores when most customers are

00:08:14.259 --> 00:08:17.610
just coming in to pick up an order or return
an item.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:21.009
Something else to consider is that, even though
Dillard’s will face less competition as

00:08:21.009 --> 00:08:25.270
competitors drop out, their real estate may
also drop in real value.

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:29.300
Virtually all of Dillard’s stores are located
in malls which garner their appeal from offering

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:31.690
customers a wide array of shopping options.

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:35.510
But as competitors go out of business, there
will be less tenants looking to lease or buy

00:08:35.510 --> 00:08:37.229
property in these malls.

00:08:37.229 --> 00:08:41.560
The properties will continue to go up in nominal
value due to inflation, but it’s very possible

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:43.930
that they start to lose real value in the
upcoming years.

00:08:43.930 --> 00:08:49.310
Also, real estate itself has gone parabolic
over the past 2 years, so as real estate cools

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:52.240
down, Dillard’s balance sheet may start
to look less bulletproof.

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:55.320
But by far the biggest concern is the supply
shortage resolving.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:09:00.519
Right now, the markets are extremely inefficient,
but this should just be temporary.

00:09:00.519 --> 00:09:04.260
Many analysts argue that it’s just a matter
of time until Dillard’s is forced to be

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:07.950
more lenient with their discounts and their
profits start to fall again to pre pandemic

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:08.950
levels.

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:13.800
At the end of the day, Dillard’s was in
a relatively strong position when the pandemic

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:14.800
hit.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:17.010
They owned their stores and they were leaning
up their business.

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:20.200
Combine this with the meme stock craze and
the supply shortage, and Dillard’s was able

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:21.330
to skyrocket 14x.

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:26.070
But, though Dillard’s has strong financials
right now, it’s still heavily debated if

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:29.620
Dillard’s will be able to carry on this
positive momentum or if this is simply their

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:32.380
last hurrah before their eventual demise.

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:33.510
Which camp are you in?

